WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.369
Let's now focus on unstructured data.

2
00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.971
You'll design two specialist agents

3
00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.173
to decide on the graph model that can

4
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.676
be extracted from markdown files. Let's get to it.

5
00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:14.347
You have built a complete workflow
for defining a construction plan

6
00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:17.617
that can build a knowledge graph
from CSV files.

7
00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.954
Now you can move on to the unstructured
data workflow.

8
00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:25.825
This starts in a similar way with user
intent and file suggestion agents,

9
00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.927
but now for markdown files.

10
00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.363
Those agents are needed
for the complete solution,

11
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:32.932
but we'll simulate their output
in this lesson.

12
00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:35.935
Instead,
you will focus on the new concept,

13
00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.805
the Entity and Fact Type Proposal Agent.

14
00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:41.274
The Entity and Fact Type Proposal Agent

15
00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:44.644
is itself composed of two dedicated sub agents,

16
00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:48.748
the named entity recognition,
or NER schema agent,

17
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.918
and the fact type extraction agent.

18
00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:55.855
Notice that the output of these agents
is a plan for how to perform

19
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.925
knowledge extraction,
not to do the extraction itself.

20
00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:02.395
The NER schema agent will read the markdown files

21
00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.330
looking for named entities.

22
00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:08.535
These are people, places,
or things that are prominent in the text.

23
00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.603
In our example, you'd expect things

24
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.739
related to furniture and reviews.

25
00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:17.077
The fact type extraction agent
is a second pass over the text

26
00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.547
that looks for the kind of statements
that appear about those things.

27
00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:24.017
So for example,
some furniture has issues.

28
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.820
Together, those agents will come up with a plan

29
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.855
for extracting information that supports

30
00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:30.356
the approved user goal.

31
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.992
To begin of course, we're going to have to

32
00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.195
go through some setup and import the usual libraries.

33
00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.163
And then we'll get OpenAI set

34
00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.965
up and check that it's working perfectly.

35
00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:43.269
That looks good. And we'll also check that Neo4J is available.

36
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.839
It's ready too. Good.

37
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.640
Now, the first agent that you're going

38
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.542
to define is a Named Entity Recognition agent.

39
00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:53.646
Named Entity Recognition is a very common
natural language processing

40
00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.249
operation that you can find in many different frameworks.

41
00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.651
It turns out, of course, that LLMs are very good with language,

42
00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.154
so they're very good with this task as well. Now,

43
00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.157
Named Entity Recognition, as it says,

44
00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:07.160
is the idea of like having entities that are recognized

45
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.229
and giving them names really.
That's that's what's kind of happening.

46
00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.830
An entity can be anything from

47
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.432
a person, a place or a thing.

48
00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:15.135
And here we're going to ask the LLM to look for things

49
00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:17.437
that are relevant to the user's goal

50
00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.539
that it can find within the text that's available.

51
00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:22.041
You'll start by defining the instructions for the agent

52
00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:23.676
and as before, we're going to break

53
00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.044
this up into a couple different parts

54
00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.280
that we'll then compose together. First, define

55
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.549
what the agent's role and goal are.

56
00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.719
Here, you're going to describe
the agent as being a top-tier algorithm

57
00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:35.522
designed for doing natural language processing really.

58
00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:39.325
And its goal is to find named entities within the text,

59
00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:41.327
but it's not actually going to extract those entities.

60
00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:43.296
The goal here is for it to

61
00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:45.031
identify what kinds of entities are available.

62
00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:47.267
You can then give some more hints

63
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.868
to the agent to let it know

64
00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.870
exactly what we're looking for here.

65
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.873
And again, we're going to describe what an entity is,

66
00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.408
and then also we're going to break

67
00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:57.143
it down to two different categories of entities.

68
00:02:57.143 --> 00:03:00.446
We're going to describe what we're calling here well-known entities,

69
00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:03.650
and these are well-known because they exist in the structured data

70
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.852
that we've already described in the previous lesson.

71
00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:08.288
So if that structured data, if some of

72
00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.390
those entities defined there occur in the text,

73
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.393
we want those parts of the text to also be extracted.

74
00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.696
In addition to that, we have what we're calling discovered entities.

75
00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.698
And these are entities that may not

76
00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.700
exist in the graph data that we've described,

77
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.203
but they might be appropriate to the user's goal.

78
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.272
And if they appear consistently,
then maybe there's something useful.

79
00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.309
We then give some more details about, you know,

80
00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:31.311
what the design rules are for identifying

81
00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:34.581
what a well-known entity is, and also what a discovered entity is.

82
00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.584
And as before, usually give a couple of examples

83
00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:40.587
to help the agent really understand what the purpose is.

84
00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.857
The final part of the instructions that you want to put together

85
00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:46.859
are the chain of thought directions

86
00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.561
and here we're going to describe exactly how

87
00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.263
to prepare for doing the task at hand,

88
00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.165
which is identifying these entities.

89
00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.968
So here's the tools that it needs to use to actually

90
00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.570
find out what is expected of it,
give it the full context.

91
00:03:58.071 --> 00:03:59.606
And then here is the proposed series

92
00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:01.174
of steps that it should go through.

93
00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.942
It knows some files that are

94
00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.577
available. It has a sampling file tool.

95
00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:05.645
So go ahead and use that to

96
00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:06.846
take a look at some of the files.

97
00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:10.850
and see what it can discover and
consider both the well-known entities

98
00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.453
and then also discovering new ones
that are frequently mentioned.

99
00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:16.356
And then through that,
put together an entire list of entities

100
00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:18.057
that it thinks are appropriate.

101
00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:21.361
And then use the get_proposed_entities tool to find that list.

102
00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:23.863
And as before, we'll turn back to the user to say,

103
00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:25.865
hey, does that look correct?

104
00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:27.500
And then there'll be a separate tool

105
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.335
that is called for actually making the approval.

106
00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.838
Here it's called the approve_proposed_entities tool.

107
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:35.141
You can then compose all those together into a single string

108
00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.277
and this will become our agent

109
00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:39.545
instructions for the named entity recognition agent.

110
00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:42.181
With the instructions defined, you can then

111
00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.150
provide the definitions of the tools themselves,

112
00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.519
and these are going to be pretty straightforward.

113
00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.521
There's not a lot of cleverness happening here.

114
00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.023
The tools we're going to define

115
00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:51.424
follow the same pattern that we've had.

116
00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.459
in previous lessons, we're going to ask

117
00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.461
the agent to first propose some things

118
00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:58.264
and then have some other tools that will take those proposals

119
00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:00.266
and turn them into approved versions of those.

120
00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.269
So here, the things we're proposing are a list of entities

121
00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.672
that are first proposed and then approved.

122
00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.007
And then you can also of course

123
00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.976
get those either proposals or the approvals.

124
00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.379
The next tool you need is a little bit different.

125
00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.381
It's going to be another getter tool.

126
00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:18.584
So this is going to be getting the well-known types

127
00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:21.087
from the schema that was proposed in the previous lesson

128
00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:23.089
from the structured data.

129
00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:25.591
So the well-known types will end up being the labels

130
00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:29.095
that were defined for nodes in the previous proposed schema.

131
00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:32.098
Here they're going to be used as the approved labels.

132
00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:34.400
We're going to pull them out of the construction plan

133
00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.602
and then just return that as a list.

134
00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.340
You'll go ahead and import the predefined tools

135
00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.208
that we've defined in previous lessons,

136
00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:44.010
and also the additional ones here.

137
00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:46.713
As before, you can combine them into a single list,

138
00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:48.414
and that's what we'll use as part of the agent definition.

139
00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:50.983
Now, to get an idea of what the agent

140
00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:53.119
is working with, you can use that sample_file function,

141
00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:55.388
just go ahead and call it directly on one of

142
00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:57.623
the available files and take a look at the content.

143
00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.827
So looking at this markdown file,

144
00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:02.128
you can see that this is

145
00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:03.629
a bunch of reviews in markdown format.

146
00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.631
It's got the headings, it's got some

147
00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:07.633
values that are embedded here like ratings.

148
00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.836
You can see there's usernames, locations,

149
00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:12.338
and of course the text of the review itself.

150
00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:16.008
So there's only a handful of reviews in here,

151
00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:18.911
but it's enough to kind of demonstrate how this all works.

152
00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:24.984
Okay, you've got your instructions defined,
you've got your tools defined.

153
00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.286
Now we can construct the agent itself.

154
00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:31.190
Now to run this agent, because it's part of a longer workflow,

155
00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:34.494
it has some assumptions about
what's really been accumulated in state.

156
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.995
So we've got to create an

157
00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.697
initial state to try this agent out.

158
00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:40.700
And the initial state requires a couple of things.

159
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.003
It needs to have the approved user goal,
the approved files.

160
00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.338
Here you can see the markdown files

161
00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:46.606
it's going to take a look at.

162
00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:48.608
And then also the construction plan.

163
00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:52.612
And it's the construction plan that's used
for those well-known entity types.

164
00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:55.114
Notice we're leaving out the relationship construction

165
00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.417
just because we don't need that in this step.

166
00:06:57.817 --> 00:07:00.019
So this is just kind of simulating what you would need.

167
00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.122
Now you're ready to go ahead and run the agent.

168
00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:06.426
We're going to use that make_agent_caller
from the helper module

169
00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:09.328
and we're going to send in a simple request here.

170
00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:11.030
We're going to just basically tell it, you

171
00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.532
know, hey agent, can you do your job?

172
00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:14.734
Add some product reviews to the knowledge graph

173
00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:17.804
to take product complaints back through the manufacturing process.

174
00:07:18.004 --> 00:07:19.372
If you run into any kind of

175
00:07:19.372 --> 00:07:20.706
trouble here, of course, you can run

176
00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:24.010
the alternative version of this, which is a very short sentence,

177
00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.645
but it has the debug, comma True

178
00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:27.213
here that'll show you all the output.

179
00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:29.382
Once the agent is done kind of getting an idea

180
00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:31.717
of what it wants to propose and it makes a proposal,

181
00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:33.653
we'll take a look at the session state to

182
00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:35.488
make sure that it actually has made a proposal

183
00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:37.990
and hopefully it should not have automatically approved.

184
00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.393
It should be waiting for us to say, yeah, that looks good.

185
00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.062
So this will take a couple minutes to run

186
00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:44.997
because the agent is going to go off, take

187
00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:46.866
a look at a couple of these different files

188
00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:48.734
and try to come up with what it

189
00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:50.770
thinks is the best set of labels to use.

190
00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:54.006
for the entities. Okay, pretty good job.

191
00:07:54.307 --> 00:07:57.877
It's identified that there's products here,
there's issues that are reported,

192
00:07:58.077 --> 00:07:59.879
complaints about customer experience,

193
00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.882
assembly instructions and maybe some customer feedback.

194
00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:04.650
Maybe a bit of redundancy, but

195
00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.085
this this looks good enough to me.

196
00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:08.421
and this is also important.

197
00:08:08.421 --> 00:08:10.122
Not only did it have a good response,

198
00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:12.124
but it also updated the session state,

199
00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:14.727
the memory correctly. So we've got proposed entities,

200
00:08:14.727 --> 00:08:16.429
but we do not yet have approval.

201
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:19.265
So I've gotten a good result here.

202
00:08:19.265 --> 00:08:21.567
If you have not, feel free to run that cell again

203
00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:24.136
and see if you get a better set of proposed entities.

204
00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:25.838
Again, this is all set up assuming that

205
00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:27.540
we're going to be in an interactive environment.

206
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.141
But if it works, you can

207
00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:30.643
go ahead and send a new message

208
00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:34.647
to the same agent saying I approve those proposed entities.

209
00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.850
Once this is done, we should find that it has actually gone

210
00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.653
and transferred the proposed entities into approved entities,

211
00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:43.089
and we should see that in

212
00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.691
the session state. Perfect. There we go.

213
00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:48.561
The approved entities matching
what they had proposed before.

214
00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:52.164
You can then move on to the second agent.

215
00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:55.968
and this is going to be our Fact Type Extraction Sub-agent.

216
00:08:56.769 --> 00:08:58.271
And as the name implies, of course,

217
00:08:58.271 --> 00:08:59.972
and similar to what the previous agent did,

218
00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:01.440
we're going to be looking for the

219
00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:02.575
types of things that can be extracted.

220
00:09:02.675 --> 00:09:04.277
We're not going to do the extraction itself.

221
00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:07.246
So you can start with the instructions,

222
00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:09.549
because that's the most important part
of both of these agents.

223
00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:11.551
And for this agent, the role and goal,

224
00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:14.754
again, we're going to say that this agent is a top-tier algorithm.

225
00:09:14.754 --> 00:09:16.756
It's going to be doing some text analysis.

226
00:09:16.756 --> 00:09:18.958
But here the goal is a little bit different.

227
00:09:18.958 --> 00:09:20.593
It's going to be looking for the

228
00:09:20.593 --> 00:09:22.261
type of facts that could be extracted.

229
00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:24.263
Don't actually extract those facts,

230
00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:26.265
just find what types of facts are possible.

231
00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:28.834
To help the agent out to

232
00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:30.703
understand what we're really talking about here.

233
00:09:30.903 --> 00:09:32.705
We're going to provide some hints, and the

234
00:09:32.705 --> 00:09:34.507
important part here that we're going to say

235
00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.709
and it's similar to what we had in the previous instructions

236
00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:40.112
for the the previous agents.
Do not propose specific individual facts,

237
00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:42.415
but instead propose the general type of fact

238
00:09:42.415 --> 00:09:44.317
that would be relevant for the user's goal.

239
00:09:44.517 --> 00:09:46.819
and giving examples is always a great idea.

240
00:09:47.119 --> 00:09:49.822
Here my example is do not propose that ABK likes coffee,

241
00:09:50.122 --> 00:09:53.826
but the general type of fact that, you know,
person likes beverage.

242
00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:56.529
So these form very simple sentences

243
00:09:56.629 --> 00:09:59.432
and we call these sentences triplets and they're of the form

244
00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:02.435
subject, predicate, object. And you can notice

245
00:10:02.535 --> 00:10:03.970
that here we've put it in parentheses,

246
00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:05.438
this is a classic thing to do.

247
00:10:05.638 --> 00:10:08.140
We'll see if the agent actually picks up on that format

248
00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.409
and provides that back to us in the response.

249
00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.112
We give it some additional design rules

250
00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:14.847
for how to think about this and what to

251
00:10:14.847 --> 00:10:16.816
look for, and then also how to use the tools.

